f 1079 #uuid "8367c446-434a-5ff8-a812-7c8889e2a163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360592 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360592 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95332" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360592 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360592 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360592 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7dc26c3f-4fa0-5dd4-9727-38908990fe9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360592 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}{v,w}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360593 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360593 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95334" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360593 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360593 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360593 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df8c26b1-92fc-50b7-bb17-353cc8004f63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360593 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186360053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95365" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360599 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\na=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{11}+{p}_{23}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b26db0ef-f872-54f3-a0bb-f12c7e09ce6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360599 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}{a}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={17}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 6 & -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{17}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 6 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 13\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{11}+{p}_{23}=8+4=12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186378045 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186378046 #edb/ref 175921860