204" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395024 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395024 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a matrix that defines the orthogonal projection of ${R}^{3}$ onto the plane whose parametric equation is $r=sv+tw$, where\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186395024 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e06ca66-7dd3-5561-91f7-cf5648b2521e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395024 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the matrix ${P}_{S}=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}$ maps any vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ onto its orthogonal projection in the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of the matrix $A.$ \n\nNotice that the plane $r=sv+tw$ is actually the subspace $\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}.$ \nHence, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 14\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -16 & 11 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 26 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 26\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186395025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186395025 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186395027 #